---
import { Code } from 'astro:components';
import SiteLayout from '../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
import { url } from '../lib/url';

const jsonToolTrace = `# Same task: "Top 3 machines by downtime in the last 30 days."
# JSON tool-call path — four model turns, four roundtrips.
# Each function_call is a structured Part the model emits; each
# function_response is the runtime's reply on the next turn.

# Turn 1 — model emits function_call.
{"function_call": {
    "name": "query_downtime_events",
    "args": {"since_days": 30}
}}
# Turn 1 — runtime dispatches, returns function_response.
{"function_response": {
    "name": "query_downtime_events",
    "response": {"rows": 14802, "ref": "ds://q-7f3a"}
}}

# Turn 2 — model decides it needs an aggregate.
{"function_call": {
    "name": "aggregate_rows",
    "args": {"ref": "ds://q-7f3a", "by": "machine_id",
             "metric": "sum", "field": "duration_minutes"}
}}
{"function_response": {
    "name": "aggregate_rows",
    "response": {"rows": 312, "ref": "ds://a-12c0"}
}}

# Turn 3 — model asks for top-N.
{"function_call": {
    "name": "top_n",
    "args": {"ref": "ds://a-12c0", "n": 3,
             "order_by": "duration_minutes", "desc": true}
}}
{"function_response": {
    "name": "top_n",
    "response": {"rows": [
        {"machine_id": "M-42", "duration_minutes": 1104.2},
        {"machine_id": "M-07", "duration_minutes":  846.5},
        {"machine_id": "M-13", "duration_minutes":  763.1}
    ]}
}}

# Turn 4 — model emits the final natural-language answer.
"M-42 leads at 18.4h, M-07 at 14.1h, M-13 at 12.7h over the last 30 days."`;

const codeActTrace = `# Same task. One model turn. One Python block.
import pandas as pd

df = run_query("""
    SELECT machine_id, duration_minutes
    FROM downtime_events
    WHERE ts >= now() - interval '30 days'
""")

top = (
    df.groupby("machine_id")["duration_minutes"]
      .sum()
      .nlargest(3)
)
print(top.to_string())
# M-42    1104.2
# M-07     846.5
# M-13     763.1`;
---

<SiteLayout current="codeact" title="CodeAct deep dive">
  <section class="section page-section">
    <div class="section-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">The action shape that scales</p>
      <h1>CodeAct: when the action <em>is</em> code, the loop gets shorter.</h1>
      <p>
        CodeAct means the model's primary action is writing and running
        executable code, instead of emitting one JSON tool call per turn.
        Every JSON tool call is a roundtrip the model has to plan for and the
        runtime has to ship a schema for. Replace the JSON with executable code
        and the model expresses composition the way an engineer would — in
        variables, loops, and function calls — collapsing several turns into
        one. The rest of this page is the evidence, the mechanism, and the
        place where the trade-off honestly breaks down.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="signal-panel signal-panel--two signal-panel--definitions" style="margin-bottom: 48px;">
      <div>
        <article>
          <span>Bounded</span>
          <strong>JSON tool calls</strong>
          <p>Pre-defined schema, one operation per call.</p>
          <ul class="use-cases">
            <li>Action space is the set of tools the developer enumerated.</li>
            <li>Every new combination is a new prompt-engineering problem.</li>
            <li>Intermediate results don't compose — each call is a roundtrip.</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article>
          <span>Open</span>
          <strong>CodeAct</strong>
          <p>Executable code as a single, unified action.</p>
          <ul class="use-cases">
            <li>Action space is whatever the language and runtime can express.</li>
            <li>Composition is free — variables, loops, and conditions are first-class.</li>
            <li>Errors come back as tracebacks: the richest feedback shape an agent gets.</li>
          </ul>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="signal-panel signal-panel--definitions" style="margin-bottom: 48px;">
      <h3>The evidence</h3>
      <div>
        <article>
          <span>Success rate</span>
          <strong>Up to 20% higher.</strong>
          <p>Than JSON or text actions across 17 LLMs (Wang et al., ICML 2024).</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <span>Turns per task</span>
          <strong>~30% fewer steps.</strong>
          <p>Than JSON, with a matching reduction in tokens generated.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <span>Adoption</span>
          <strong>Three major vendors.</strong>
          <p>Microsoft Agent Framework (early 2026), Google ADK code executors, OpenAI's code interpreter — same idea, three vendors.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-head compact-head" style="margin-top: 36px;">
      <p class="eyebrow">The contrast, in code</p>
      <h2>Same task, two action shapes.</h2>
      <p>
        The JSON side below shows the actual wire shape: the model emits a
        <code>function_call</code> part, the runtime dispatches it, and a
        <code>function_response</code> comes back on the next turn. (For the
        mechanism in detail, see <a href={url('/function-tools/')}>chapter 03A</a>.)
        Compare it against the CodeAct side, where the same four turns collapse
        into one Python block.
      </p>
    </div>

    <article class="harness-row">
      <div class="harness-row__intro">
        <span>JSON tool calls</span>
        <h3>Four turns. Four function_calls. Four schemas the developer maintains.</h3>
        <p>
          Each operation is a separate model decision wrapped in a separate
          tool. The agent can only do what the tools allow, in the order the
          schemas permit. Adding a new aggregation means shipping a new tool.
        </p>
      </div>
      <figure class="code-card">
        <figcaption>
          <span class="lang-pill">python</span>
          <span class="code-filename">json_tool_trace.py</span>
          <span class="code-tag">wire-shape trace</span>
        </figcaption>
        <Code code={jsonToolTrace} lang="python" theme="github-light" />
      </figure>
    </article>

    <article class="harness-row">
      <div class="harness-row__intro">
        <span>CodeAct</span>
        <h3>One turn. One Python block. Composition is free.</h3>
        <p>
          The agent writes the analysis the way an analyst would. The action
          surface is pandas + the language. Intermediate results live in
          variables, not in JSON envelopes. The model sees the result and
          summarizes — no extra round trip.
        </p>
      </div>
      <figure class="code-card">
        <figcaption>
          <span class="lang-pill">python</span>
          <span class="code-filename">codeact_trace.py</span>
          <span class="code-tag">illustrative trace</span>
        </figcaption>
        <Code code={codeActTrace} lang="python" theme="github-light" />
      </figure>
    </article>

    <div class="section-head compact-head" style="margin-top: 36px;">
      <p class="eyebrow">Honest limits</p>
      <h2>Where CodeAct isn't the answer.</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="signal-panel signal-panel--definitions" style="margin-bottom: 48px;">
      <div>
        <article>
          <span>Trust boundary</span>
          <strong>The action surface is "anything the runtime can do."</strong>
          <p>That's the point — and the risk. CodeAct requires a real sandbox: no ambient credentials, no network unless granted, no filesystem outside a working directory. ADK's <code>AgentEngineSandboxCodeExecutor</code> handles this; rolling your own is harder than it looks.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <span>Auditability</span>
          <strong>Free-form code is harder to permission than enumerated tools.</strong>
          <p>When every action is a typed tool call, a compliance reviewer can read the tool list. When actions are arbitrary code, the eval trace becomes the audit surface. Workable, but it shifts where the burden sits.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <span>Model skill ceiling</span>
          <strong>Small models write worse code than they fill JSON.</strong>
          <p>The CodeAct paper measured across 17 LLMs. Larger and code-tuned models showed the largest gains; smaller general models did better with structured JSON. The trade-off shifts with the model you ship.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>


  </section>
</SiteLayout>
